  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nRain continues through Wednesday morning. Some drought \nrelief is possible across Northern Maine with amounts up to 1\ninch.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA stationary front is currently stalled over the area resulting\nin light rain over much of central Maine. Light rain is expected\nto continue through tonight and become more steady as a northern\nstream shortwave trough approaches from the west, providing \nadditional lift along the boundary as it pushes north as a warm \nfront through Wednesday. Precipitation should end early tomorrow\nmorning in southern areas but may linger through the early \nafternoon in far northern Maine.\n\nThe greatest precipitation amounts are expected to be in\nnorthern Maine where totals should approach 1 inch in\ncorrelation with where the greatest forcing associated with the\nnorthern stream trough will be. Progressively lesser amounts\nfurther south with only around 0.1 inches in the Bangor and\nDowneast areas. Some limited drought relief is expected over\nnorthern areas but will not be enough to end the significant\ndeficit.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nAdditional rainfall expected Thursday into the weekend, though \nexact timing and amounts are still uncertain. The rainfall could\nprovide some drought relief.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will be located southeast of the Gulf of Maine \nThursday while an occluded front crosses the region. At the same \ntime, low pressure will move northeast from Great Lakes along a\ncold front. The return flow between the high and occluded front\nwill help draw moisture northward across the region with \nprecipitable water values increasing to 1.25 to 1.50 inches. \nExpect a chance of rain across mostly northern and western \nportions of the forecast area Thursday. The cold front will \napproach the Saint Lawrence Valley Thursday night. A slight \nchance/chance of rain or showers will linger Thursday night. An \nupper low approaches from the Great Lakes Friday helping support\na developing coastal low, though the exact location of the \ndeveloping low is still uncertain. The better chances of rain \nlater Friday will occur across Downeast area. The upper/surface \nlows should approach Friday night. Steadier rain should begin \nto expand northward across the region Friday night. The surface \nlow lifts across the region Saturday into Saturday night with \nthe area of rain lifting across the forecast area. The low lifts\nnorth of the forecast area Sunday. Showers wrapping around the \nlow could persist across the region Sunday. Precipitation totals\nThursday through Sunday remain uncertain dependent on the track\nand evolution of the system.",
